#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:1
msgid "Putting It All Together: Futures, Tasks, and Threads"
msgstr "综合概述：未来、任务与线程"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:3
msgid ""
"As we saw in [Chapter 16](http://localhost:3000/ch16-00-concurrency."
"html)<!-- ignore -->, threads provide one approach to concurrency. We’ve "
"seen another approach in this chapter: using async with futures and streams. "
"If you’re wondering when to choose one method over the other, the answer is: "
"it depends! And in many cases, the choice isn’t threads _or_ async but "
"rather threads _and_ async."
msgstr ""
"正如我们在 [Chapter 16](http://localhost:3000/ch16-00-concurrency.html)<!-- "
"ignore --> 中看到的，线程提供了一种实现并发的方式。在本章中，我们还介绍了另一"
"种实现并发的方法：使用异步函数和流。如果你想知道何时应该选择其中一种方法，答"
"案是：这取决于具体情况！在许多情况下，选择不是线程_或_异步，而是线程_和_异"
"步。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:9
msgid ""
"Many operating systems have supplied threading-based concurrency models for "
"decades now, and many programming languages support them as a result. "
"However, these models are not without their tradeoffs. On many operating "
"systems, they use a fair bit of memory for each thread. Threads are also "
"only an option when your operating system and hardware support them. Unlike "
"mainstream desktop and mobile computers, some embedded systems don’t have an "
"OS at all, so they also don’t have threads."
msgstr ""
"许多操作系统已经以基于线程的并发模型为基础运行了数十年，因此许多编程语言也支"
"持这些模型。然而，这些模型并非没有缺点。在许多操作系统中，每个线程会占用相当"
"多的内存资源。而且，线程只有在操作系统和硬件支持的情况下才可用。与主流台式机"
"和移动计算机不同，一些嵌入式系统根本就没有操作系统，因此也就没有线程。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:17
msgid ""
"The async model provides a different—and ultimately complementary—set of "
"tradeoffs. In the async model, concurrent operations don’t require their own "
"threads. Instead, they can run on tasks, as when we used `trpl::spawn_task` "
"to kick off work from a synchronous function in the streams section. A task "
"is similar to a thread, but instead of being managed by the operating "
"system, it’s managed by library-level code: the runtime."
msgstr ""
"异步模型提供了一组不同的——且最终是互补的——权衡方案。在异步模型中，并发操作不"
"需要自己的线程。相反，它们可以在任务上运行，就像我们在 streams 部分使用 "
"`trpl::spawn_task` 来从同步函数开始执行工作那样。任务类似于线程，但与其由操作"
"系统管理，不如说是由库级代码——即运行时——来管理的。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:24
msgid ""
"There’s a reason the APIs for spawning threads and spawning tasks are so "
"similar. Threads act as a boundary for sets of synchronous operations; "
"concurrency is possible _between_ threads. Tasks act as a boundary for sets "
"of _asynchronous_ operations; concurrency is possible both _between_ and "
"_within_ tasks, because a task can switch between futures in its body. "
"Finally, futures are Rust’s most granular unit of concurrency, and each "
"future may represent a tree of other futures. The runtime—specifically, its "
"executor—manages tasks, and tasks manage futures. In that regard, tasks are "
"similar to lightweight, runtime-managed threads with added capabilities that "
"come from being managed by a runtime instead of by the operating system."
msgstr ""
"线程和任务相关的 API 之所以如此相似，是有原因的。线程充当同步操作集合的边界；"
"并发可以在线程之间实现。而任务则充当异步操作集合的边界；并发既可以在任务之间"
"实现，也可以在任务内部实现，因为任务可以在其执行过程中切换不同的未来操作。最"
"后，未来操作是 Rust 中最细粒度的并发单位，每个未来操作可能代表着其他未来操作"
"的树状结构。运行时——具体来说，就是它的执行器——负责管理任务，而任务则负责管理"
"未来操作。从这个角度来看，任务类似于由运行时管理的轻量级线程，只不过它们还具"
"备一些额外的功能，这些功能是由运行时而不是操作系统来管理的。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:35
msgid ""
"This doesn’t mean that async tasks are always better than threads (or vice "
"versa). Concurrency with threads is in some ways a simpler programming model "
"than concurrency with `async`. That can be a strength or a weakness. Threads "
"are somewhat “fire and forget”; they have no native equivalent to a future, "
"so they simply run to completion without being interrupted except by the "
"operating system itself."
msgstr ""
"这并不意味着异步任务总是优于线程（或者相反）。在某些方面，使用线程进行并发处"
"理比使用 `async` 进行并发处理更为简单。这既可以是优势，也可能是劣势。线程的运"
"作方式较为“点火后即可放弃”；它们没有类似于未来的概念，因此线程会一直运行到结"
"束，除非被操作系统本身中断。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:42
msgid ""
"And it turns out that threads and tasks often work very well together, "
"because tasks can (at least in some runtimes) be moved around between "
"threads. In fact, under the hood, the runtime we’ve been using—including the "
"`spawn_blocking` and `spawn_task` functions—is multithreaded by default! "
"Many runtimes use an approach called _work stealing_ to transparently move "
"tasks around between threads, based on how the threads are currently being "
"utilized, to improve the system’s overall performance. That approach "
"actually requires threads _and_ tasks, and therefore futures."
msgstr ""
"事实证明，线程和任务通常可以很好地协同工作，因为任务可以在(某些运行时环境中)"
"在多个线程之间移动。实际上，在我们所使用的运行时环境中——包括 "
"`spawn_blocking` 和 `spawn_task` 函数——默认情况下就是多线程的！许多运行时环境"
"采用一种称为“工作窃取”的方法，根据线程当前的使用情况来透明地移动任务，从而提"
"高系统的整体性能。这种方法实际上需要线程以及任务，因此也需要使用未来函数。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:51
msgid ""
"When thinking about which method to use when, consider these rules of thumb:"
msgstr "在考虑使用哪种方法时，可以参考以下经验法则："

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:53
msgid ""
"If the work is _very parallelizable_ (that is, CPU-bound), such as "
"processing a bunch of data where each part can be processed separately, "
"threads are a better choice."
msgstr ""
"如果任务具有**高度并行性**（即依赖CPU处理），比如处理大量数据，且每个部分都可"
"以单独处理，那么使用线程是一个更好的选择。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:56
msgid ""
"If the work is _very concurrent_ (that is, I/O-bound), such as handling "
"messages from a bunch of different sources that may come in at different "
"intervals or different rates, async is a better choice."
msgstr ""
"如果任务具有**高度并发性**（即依赖I/O操作），比如处理来自多个不同来源的消息，"
"这些消息可能以不同的时间间隔或速率到达，那么使用异步编程是一个更好的选择。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:60
msgid ""
"And if you need both parallelism and concurrency, you don’t have to choose "
"between threads and async. You can use them together freely, letting each "
"play the part it’s best at. For example, Listing 17-25 shows a fairly common "
"example of this kind of mix in real-world Rust code."
msgstr ""
"如果你需要并行性和并发性，你不必在线程和异步之间做出选择。你可以自由地将它们"
"结合起来使用，让每个技术特性发挥它最擅长的功能。例如，清单17-25展示了现实世界"
"Rust代码中这种混合使用的常见例子。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:68
msgid "// for mdbook test\n"
msgstr "// mdbook 测试\n"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:84
msgid "\"{message}\""
msgstr "\"{message}\""

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:92
msgid ""
"We begin by creating an async channel, then spawning a thread that takes "
"ownership of the sender side of the channel using the `move` keyword. Within "
"the thread, we send the numbers 1 through 10, sleeping for a second between "
"each. Finally, we run a future created with an async block passed to `trpl::"
"block_on` just as we have throughout the chapter. In that future, we await "
"those messages, just as in the other message-passing examples we have seen."
msgstr ""
"我们首先创建一个异步通道，然后创建一个线程，该线程使用 `move` 关键字来负责通"
"道的发送端。在該线程中，我们依次发送数字 1 到 10，每次发送之间休息一秒。最"
"后，我们运行一个由 async 块创建的未来对象，该对象通过 `trpl::block_on` 传递给"
"线程。在该未来中，我们等待这些消息的到来，就像我们在本章中看到的其他消息传递"
"示例一样。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:100
msgid ""
"To return to the scenario we opened the chapter with, imagine running a set "
"of video encoding tasks using a dedicated thread (because video encoding is "
"compute-bound) but notifying the UI that those operations are done with an "
"async channel. There are countless examples of these kinds of combinations "
"in real-world use cases."
msgstr ""
"回到我们开始讨论的场景，假设我们使用一个专用线程来执行视频编码任务（因为视频"
"编码需要大量的计算资源），同时通过异步通道向用户界面通知这些操作已经完成。在"
"现实世界的用例中，这种组合方式有很多例子。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:106
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "摘要"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:108
msgid ""
"This isn’t the last you’ll see of concurrency in this book. The project in "
"[Chapter 21](ch21-00-final-project-a-web-server.html)<!-- ignore --> will "
"apply these concepts in a more realistic situation than the simpler examples "
"discussed here and compare problem-solving with threading versus tasks and "
"futures more directly."
msgstr ""
"这并非本书中关于并发性的最后一部分内容。[Chapter 21](ch21-00-final-project-a-"
"web-server.html)<!-- ignore -->这个项目将把这些概念应用于更现实的情况中，并且"
"会更直接地比较使用线程与任务和未来任务进行问题解决的差异。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:113
msgid ""
"No matter which of these approaches you choose, Rust gives you the tools you "
"need to write safe, fast, concurrent code—whether for a high-throughput web "
"server or an embedded operating system."
msgstr ""
"无论您选择哪种方法，Rust都能为您提供编写安全、高效、可并发处理的代码所需的工"
"具——无论是用于高吞吐量网络服务器还是嵌入式操作系统。"

#: src/ch17-06-futures-tasks-threads.md:117
msgid ""
"Next, we’ll talk about idiomatic ways to model problems and structure "
"solutions as your Rust programs get bigger. In addition, we’ll discuss how "
"Rust’s idioms relate to those you might be familiar with from object-"
"oriented programming."
msgstr ""
"接下来，我们将讨论在Rust程序中处理复杂问题时惯用的编程方式，以及如何构建解决"
"方案。此外，我们还将探讨Rust的惯用法与您可能熟悉的面向对象编程中的惯用法之间"
"的关系。"
